&Q000784

{1 = 1}
9=1.	@wPN{Ud-ul-uru} (Old man cultivator) gave advice to his son: 

{2 = 2-7}
15=2.	When you have to prepare a field, inspect the levees, 
19=3.	canals and mounds that have to be opened. When you let the flood water into the field, 
23=4.	this water should not rise too high in it. 
27=5.	At the time that the field emerges from the water, 
31=6.	watch its area with standing water; it should be fenced. 
35=7.	Do not let cattle herds trample there. 

{8 = 8-13}
39=8.	After you cut back the plant bundles and establish the limits of the field, 
43=9.	level it repeatedly with a thin hoe weighing two-thirds of a mina @note{approx. 650 g}. Let a flat hoe erase the oxen tracks, 
47=10.	let the field be swept clean. 
51=11.	A maul should flatten the furrow bottoms of the area. 
55=12.	A hoe should go round the four edges of the field. 
59=13.	Until the field is dry it should be smoothed out. 

{14 = 14-22}
63=14.	Your implements should be ready. The parts of your yoke should be assembled. 
67=15.	Your new whip should hang from a nail -- the bindings of the handle of your old whip should be repaired by artisans. 
71=16.	The adze, drill and saw, your tools and your strength, 
75=17.	should be in good order. Let braided thongs, straps, 
79=18.	leather wrappings and whips be attached securely. 
83=19.	Let your sowing basket be checked, 
87=20.	and its sides made strong. 
91=21.	What you need for the field should be at hand. 
95=22.	Inspect your work carefully. 

{23 = 23-29}
99=23.	The plough oxen will have back-up oxen. 
103=24.	The attachments of ox to ox should be loose. 
107=25.	Each plough will have a back-up plough. 
111=26.	The assigned task for one plough  
115=27.	is 180 @fsux{iku} @note{approx. 65 ha}, but if  
119=28.	you build the implement at 144 @fsux{iku} @note{approx. 52 ha}, the work will be pleasantly performed for you. 
123=29.	180 (?) @fsux{sila} of grain @note{approx. 180 litres; expressed in the cuneiform as 3 of an unwritten measure comprising 60 @fsux{sila}, @fsux{gur} indicating that the preceding signs specify a capacity} will be spent on each 18 @fsux{iku} area @note{approx. 6 1/2 ha}. 

{30 = 30-34}
127=30.	After working one plough's area with a @fsux{bardili} plough, 
131=31.	and after working the @fsux{bardili} plough's area with a @fsux{tugsaga} plough, 
135=32.	till it with the @fsux{tuggur} plough. Harrow once, twice, three times. 
139=33.	When you flatten the stubborn spots with a heavy maul, the handle of your maul should be securely attached, 
143=34.	otherwise it will not perform as needed. 

{35 = 35-40}
147=35.	When your field work becomes excessive, 
151=36.	you should not neglect your work; 
155=37.	no one should have to tell anyone else: 
159=38.	@q[unknown,unknown]{“Do your field work!”}. When the constellations in the sky are right, 
163=39.	do not be reluctant to take the oxen force to the field many times. 
167=40.	The hoe should work everything. 

{41 = 41-45}
171=41.	When you have to work the field with the seeder-plough, your plough should be properly adjusted. 
175=42.	Put a leather sealing on the @fsux{kašu} of your plough. 
179=43.	Provide your beam with narrow pegs. 
183=44.	Your boards should be spread. 
187=45.	Make your furrows. 

{46 = 46-54}
191=46.	Make eight furrows per @fsux{nindan} @note{6 m} of width; 
195=47.	the barley will lodge in more closely spaced furrows. 
199=48.	When you have to work the field with the seeder-plough, 
203=49.	keep your eye on your man who drops the seed. 
207=50.	The grain should fall two fingers deep. 
211=51.	You should put one @fsux{giŋ} of seed per @fsux{nindan} @note{approx. 3 ml per m}. If the barley seed is not being inserted in the hollow of the furrow, 
215=52.	change the wedge of your plough share. 
219=53.	If the bindings become loose, 
223=54.	tighten them. 

{55 = 55-63}
227=55.	Where you have made vertical furrows, make slanted furrows, 
231=56.	and where you have made slanted furrows, make vertical furrows. 
235=57.	Straight furrows will give you edges that are wide enough and nice (?). 
239=58.	Your crooked furrows should be straightened out. Make the furrows clear. 
243=59.	Plough your portion of field. 
247=60.	The clods should be picked out. 
251=61.	The furrows should be made wide where the soil is open, 
255=62.	and the furrows should be narrower where the soil is clogged: 
259=63.	it is good for the seedlings. 

{64 = 64-73}
263=64.	After the seedlings break open the ground, perform the rites against mice. 
267=65.	Turn away the teeth of the locusts. 
271=66.	When the plants overflow the narrow bottoms of the furrows, 
275=67.	water them with the water of the first seed. 
279=68.	When the plants resemble a …… reed mat, water them. 
283=69.	Water the plants when they are heading. 
287=70.	When the plants are fully leafed out, 
291=71.	do not water them or they will become infected by leaf rust. 
295=72.	When the barley is right for husking, water it. 
299=73.	It will provide a yield increase of one @fsux{sila} per @fsux{ban} @note{approx. 1 litre in 10}. 

{74 = 74-80}
303=74.	When you have to reap the barley, do not let the plants become overripe. 
307=75.	Harvest at the right time. One man is to cut the barley, 
311=76.	and one to tie the sheaves; 
315=77.	and one before him should apportion the sheaves: 
319=78.	three men should harvest for you. 
323=79.	The people who gather the barley should not let the grain dry. 
327=80.	They should not scatter the grain when it is in the stacks. 

{81 = 81-90}
331=81.	Your daily work starts at daybreak. 
335=82.	Gather your force of helpers and grain gatherers  
339=83.	in sufficient number and lay down the sheaves. 
343=84.	Your work should be carefully done. 
347=85.	Although they have been having stale coarse flour, 
351=86.	do not let anyone thresh for your  
355=87.	new bread -- let the sheaves have a rest. 
359=88.	The rites for the sheaves should be performed daily. 
363=89.	When you transport your barley, 
367=90.	your barley carriers should handle small amounts (?). 

{91 = 91-95}
371=91.	Mark the limits of a vacant lot of yours. 
375=92.	Establish properly your access paths to it. 
379=93.	Your waggons should be in working order. 
383=94.	Feed the waggon's oxen. 
387=95.	Your implements should be ……. 

{96 = 96-103}
391=96.	Let your prepared threshing floor rest for a few days. 
395=97.	When you open the threshing floor, smooth its surface (?). 
399=98.	When you thresh, 
403=99.	the teeth of your threshing sledge and its leather straps should be secured with bitumen. 
407=100.	When you make the oxen trample the grain, 
411=101.	your threshers should be strong. 
415=102.	When your grain is spread on the ground, 
419=103.	perform the rites of the grain not yet clean. 

{104 = 104-106}
423=104.	When you winnow, 
427=105.	put an intelligent person as your second winnower. 
431=106.	Two people should work at moving the grain around. 

{107 = 107-109}
435=107.	When the grain is clean, lay it under the measuring stick. 
439=108.	Perform the rites in the evening and at night. 
443=109.	Release the grain at midday. 

{110 = 110-111}
447=110.	Instructions of the god @wDN{Ninurta}, the son of @wDN{Enlil} -- @wDN{Ninurta}, 
451=111.	faithful farmer of @wDN{Enlil}, your praise be good! 
